{"duration": 60.319, "exact_word_timing": true, "lines": [{"beat": 0, "text": "A fireman lowered a scoop into a trough while his train kept moving.", "start": 0.08, "end": 3.554}, {"beat": 1, "text": "One long run used 1,800 to 1,900 gallons of water.", "start": 3.974, "end": 8.951}, {"beat": 1, "text": "In rough weather, that climbed to 2,400 gallons.", "start": 9.171, "end": 12.502}, {"beat": 1, "text": "The largest tenders held only 2,000 gallons.", "start": 12.722, "end": 15.581}, {"beat": 1, "text": "Other railroad companies were slow to adopt the new device.", "start": 15.801, "end": 18.923}, {"beat": 2, "text": "The locomotive passes over the trough.", "start": 19.343, "end": 21.239}, {"beat": 2, "text": "The fireman lowers the scoop just before the start.", "start": 21.459, "end": 23.838}, {"beat": 2, "text": "Forward motion forces water up the pipe into the tank.", "start": 24.058, "end": 26.919}, {"beat": 2, "text": "Raise the scoop too late, and water soaks the tender and footplate.", "start": 27.139, "end": 30.343}, {"beat": 3, "text": "You see, it's like refilling a water bottle while you're still running.", "start": 30.763, "end": 33.968}, {"beat": 4, "text": "John Ramsbottom proved the method in 1860.", "start": 34.388, "end": 36.974}, {"beat": 4, "text": "At 15 miles an hour, motion lifted water 7\u00bd feet.", "start": 37.194, "end": 40.75}, {"beat": 4, "text": "That height reached the top of the delivery pipe.", "start": 40.97, "end": 43.106}, {"beat": 4, "text": "So trains could skip the water stop and keep their speed.", "start": 43.326, "end": 46.01}, {"beat": 4, "text": "In America, big eastern railroads like New York Central and Pennsylvania used them.", "start": 46.23, "end": 50.953}, {"beat": 5, "text": "The scoop sprayed water over passengers, so guards warned them to close the windows.", "start": 51.373, "end": 55.663}, {"beat": 5, "text": "In America, some troughs filled with rainwater and melting snow.", "start": 55.883, "end": 59.199}],